# Deploy

What the API and web images require to run, and what the entrypoint does at boot. Where
and how you actually deploy them (cloud/VM, orchestrator, DNS/CDN) is your own operational
decision — see [*Your provider*](#your-provider) below.

The local environment uses none of this — see [`local-environment.md`](local-environment.md).

## Topology

| | Staging | Production |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Front | `dev.{{ app_domain }}` | `{{ app_domain }}` |
| API | `dev.api.{{ app_domain }}` | `api.{{ app_domain }}` |

Two long-lived environments is a convention, not a requirement — adjust it to what your
provider actually offers. Whatever you run, **no resource is shared between environments**:
not the database, not secrets, not the deploy credential.

## What gets built

Two images, built from the repository.

**`apps/api/Dockerfile`** — multi-stage on `node:22-slim`: `turbo prune` isolates the
`api` subgraph, compiles, installs production dependencies only and copies `dist` into
the runner. Exposes port **3000**, enters through `tini` → `docker-entrypoint.sh` and runs
`node dist/main`.

{% if web_stack == 'next' %}
**`apps/web/Dockerfile`** — multi-stage on `node:22-alpine`: `turbo prune web --docker`
isolates the front subgraph, installs, compiles with `next build` (`output: "standalone"`)
and copies `.next/standalone`, `.next/static` and `public` into the runner, which runs as
the non-root `nextjs` user. Exposes port **3000**, with no nginx — the standalone's own
`server.js` serves assets and routes: `CMD ["node", "apps/web/server.js"]`.

> **`NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` is a build arg, not a runtime variable.** The value is baked into
> the bundle during the Next build (`ARG` → `ENV` so Next can see it and so BuildKit
> invalidates the cache when the URL changes). Changing the API URL requires a **front
> rebuild** — changing the variable and restarting the container has no effect whatsoever.
{% else %}
**`apps/web/Dockerfile`** — compiles with Vite and serves the static output on
`nginx-unprivileged`, port **8080**, with a healthcheck at `/healthz`.

> **`VITE_API_URL` is a build arg, not a runtime variable.** The value is baked into the
> bundle during the build (`ARG` → `ENV` so Vite can see it and so BuildKit invalidates
> the cache when the URL changes). Changing the API URL requires a **front rebuild** —
> changing the variable and restarting the container has no effect whatsoever.
{% endif %}

## What runs at API boot

`docker-entrypoint.sh` runs two conditional steps before bringing the app up:

| Step | Condition | Default |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Migrations (`node dist/db/migrate`) | `RUN_MIGRATIONS != false` | **on** |
| Module boot seed (`dist/modules/*/seeds/bootstrap.js`, one per installed module) | `MASTER_EMAIL` **and** `MASTER_PASSWORD` set | conditional |

Migrations are fail-fast: a wrong schema aborts the boot. `RUN_MIGRATIONS=false` only
makes sense when a dedicated job migrates and the replicas only serve.

The module boot seed replaces the old single template script: the entrypoint globs every
installed module's own `seeds/bootstrap.js` (e.g. `identity`'s superuser) and runs each one.
Every seed under this glob is idempotent (`onConflictDoNothing` by email) and safe on every
boot and per replica. Without the secrets it becomes a no-op — the dev/demo seeds (`db:seed`,
`db:seed:demo`) never run here, only what the glob finds under `dist/modules/*/seeds/`.

## Variables per environment

What differs between staging and production, and must never be copied from one to the
other:

| Variable | Note |
| --- | --- |
| `DATABASE_URL`, `REDIS_URL` | point at the environment's own services |
| `WEB_ORIGIN` | the front's exact origin; CORS mirrors only that one |
| `VITE_API_URL` (front) | build arg — see the warning above |
| `PASSWORD_PEPPER`, `CSRF_SECRET` | **generate new ones per environment**; never reuse one environment's in another |
| `MASTER_EMAIL`, `MASTER_PASSWORD` | initial superuser credential |
| `DATABASE_POOL_MAX` | ceiling of the application pool's connections; saturated → `PoolSaturatedError`, **503** with `Retry-After` (ADR 0089) |
| `DATABASE_POOL_MAX_WAITING` | ceiling of the wait queue for a connection; above it, acquisition is refused immediately (same 503) instead of queueing |
| `DATABASE_IDLE_IN_TX_TIMEOUT_MS` | ceiling for an open, idle transaction; above it Postgres terminates the session |

The full variable list, with defaults, is `apps/api/.env.example` — this table only calls
out the ones that must differ per environment. The rest (session parameters, Argon2,
lockout, cookie, timeouts) is pure configuration and can be identical in both. Validation
is fail-fast per module: a missing value or wrong format brings the boot down with an
explicit error.

## Fail-closed configuration

A batch of previously-defaulted variables now either has no default (the boot fails without
an explicit value) or defaults to the safer side and needs an explicit opt-in for the riskier
one. Same value in both environments unless noted:

| Variable | Default | Production guidance |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `TRUST_PROXY_HOPS` | `0` | Set this to the number of reverse proxies in front of the app (for example `2` behind a CDN plus your orchestrator's own edge). `0` means "trust nothing in front" — every request looks like it comes from the edge, which breaks per-IP rate limiting and records the wrong IP in `auth_events`/sessions/new-device e-mail. Never leave the default in a deployed environment that sits behind a proxy. |
| `DOCS_ENABLED` | `false` | Leave off in production (`/docs` was reachable by default before this default flipped). Turn on only for a controlled window if the OpenAPI UI is needed there. |
| `REDIS_ALLOW_PLAINTEXT` | `false` | `REDIS_URL` starting with `redis://` (not `rediss://`) in production is refused at boot unless this is `true`. Prefer `rediss://` when the Redis service supports TLS; set this flag only when the connection is already isolated to a private network. |
| `DATABASE_SSL_CA` | unset | Optional PEM (accepts escaped `\n`) for `DATABASE_SSL=require`. Unset relies on the platform's default CA trust. |
| `OUTBOX_DEAD_RETENTION_DAYS` | `30` | How long a dead-lettered outbox event survives before the `outbox-dead.purge` maintenance job removes it (`lockId` 6 — see `docs/dev/template-changelog.md`). Raise it if dead letters need a longer investigation window. |
| `STORAGE_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS` | `30000` | Timeout per request to the object storage (R2) adapter. |
| `STORAGE_MAX_SOCKETS` | `50` | Ceiling on concurrent HTTPS connections (`keepAlive` agent) to the object storage — part of the fix for the socket-leak finding that could take the whole storage subsystem down under an upload/download burst. |
| `BREACH_CHECK_ENABLED` (identity) | none — **required** | Boot fails without it. `true` calls the real HIBP breach-check adapter; `false` is a deliberate, explicit opt-out — never an accidental default. |
| `LOGIN_ACCOUNT_MAX_FAILURES` / `LOGIN_ACCOUNT_WINDOW_SECONDS` (identity) | `10` / `900` | Per-account login throttle (sums every source IP), independent from the per-IP rate limit. |
| `ATTACHMENT_PENDING_QUOTA_BYTES` / `ATTACHMENT_MAX_CONCURRENT_UPLOADS` (attachment) | `2147483648` (2 GiB) / `16` | Cap on unconfirmed pending bytes per owner and on concurrent multipart uploads per instance — both contain the storage exhaustion / socket-leak class of finding. |

## Deploy flow

`.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs three gates on every PR and on push to `main`: `quality`
(lint + typecheck + builds), `test-unit` (api + web) and `test-coverage` (api integration +
e2e, testcontainers) — see [`workflow.md`](../agents/workflow.md) for the full pipeline.
Nothing builds or deploys the images before those pass.

How a green push actually becomes a running deployment — which provider, which webhook or
CLI, which orchestrator — is whatever hook your environment configures; describe it under
*Your provider*.

## Known pitfalls

**Never deploy a long-lived environment with `Dockerfile.dev`.** `docker-entrypoint.dev.sh`
applies migrations, but does **not** run `bootstrap-master` — the environment comes up
with zero users and nobody can authenticate. On top of that it runs `pnpm dev` (ts-node in
watch mode) instead of the compiled build. `.dev` exists for local development, not for
staging or production.

**Migrations can be silently skipped on a long-lived database.** Drizzle's migrator only
runs entries whose timestamp is greater than the last applied one. If a migration lands on
`main` with a timestamp earlier than what the environment has already applied — which
happens when parallel branches create migrations with the same number and get merged out
of order — it is ignored on every boot, forever, and a redeploy does not fix it.

Before assuming an environment is up to date, compare against the journal:

```sql
SELECT count(*) FROM drizzle.__drizzle_migrations;
```

The total must match the number of entries in
`apps/api/drizzle/migrations/meta/_journal.json`. If it differs, the database has drift —
and a staging database with drift **is not a valid source for copying data** into
production.

Migrations and the module boot seed can also be fired by hand from the already-built image,
without a redeploy:

```bash
docker run --rm <api-image> node dist/db/migrate
docker run --rm -e MASTER_EMAIL=... -e MASTER_PASSWORD=... <api-image> \
  node dist/modules/identity/seeds/bootstrap.js
```

## Your provider

Product-owned: the template ships no cloud account, VM, deploy orchestrator or panel —
those are this product's own choice. Document here how a push actually reaches a running
environment (the orchestrator or CLI, the webhook or GitHub App wiring, TLS/DNS, and how an
operator reaches the VM and the database for each environment), following the shape of
[`../agents/infra.md`](../agents/infra.md).
